# Localization

## Overview

The website is currently a Persian-first corporate website.

The primary language is Persian (`fa`), and the primary text direction is right-to-left (`RTL`).

The architecture must, however, be designed so that English (`en`) can be added later without requiring a major redesign of the application, content model, URL structure, or components.

Localization should remain simple until additional languages are actually required.

---

## Current Language

The current production language is:

```text
Language: Persian
Locale: fa-IR
Direction: RTL
```

All current public-facing content should be written in Persian.

Do not introduce English placeholder content simply to demonstrate multilingual support.

---

## Future Language

The architecture should be capable of supporting:

```text
Persian: fa-IR
English: en
```

English is a future requirement, not necessarily part of the initial implementation.

Do not build a complex runtime translation system unless it becomes necessary.

The current website is primarily a static Astro website, so localization should take advantage of Astro's static generation model.

---

## Direction

Persian pages must use:

```html
<html lang="fa" dir="rtl">
```

English pages must use:

```html
<html lang="en" dir="ltr">
```

The document language and direction must be set at the page/layout level.

Do not hardcode `dir="rtl"` into individual components.

Components must work correctly in both RTL and LTR layouts.

---

## RTL Requirements

The UI must be designed for RTL from the beginning.

Avoid assumptions such as:

```css
margin-left: ...
padding-right: ...
left: 0;
right: 0;
text-align: left;
```

when the intention is related to logical layout direction.

Prefer CSS logical properties:

```css
margin-inline-start
margin-inline-end
padding-inline-start
padding-inline-end
inset-inline-start
inset-inline-end
text-align: start
border-start-start-radius
```

Use logical properties wherever practical.

This allows the same components to work in both RTL and LTR.

---

## Typography

Persian and Latin text must render correctly.

The design system should support:

* Persian typography
* Latin typography
* Persian/Latin mixed content
* Numbers
* URLs
* Email addresses
* Code snippets
* Product names
* Technical terminology

The font stack should be centralized in the design system.

Do not specify fonts independently inside individual components.

---

## Persian Content Rules

All current marketing and informational content should be written in natural, professional Persian.

Avoid:

* Machine-translated language
* Unnecessary English words
* Literal translations
* Excessive technical jargon
* Inconsistent terminology

Technical product names such as `GammaDesk`, `ITIL`, `API`, and similar established terms may remain in English where appropriate.

---

## Mixed Persian and English Content

The website will contain mixed-direction content.

Examples:

```text
GammaDesk IT Service Management
Incident Management در GammaDesk
ITIL 4
API Documentation
```

The UI must handle mixed Persian and English text without visual corruption.

For isolated LTR content, use appropriate direction handling rather than changing the direction of the entire page.

Examples include:

* Email addresses
* URLs
* Domain names
* Product names
* Version numbers
* Technical identifiers
* Code
* English quotations

---

## Numbers

The application may contain both Persian and Latin numbers.

Do not globally transform numbers without a clear content requirement.

Technical and business identifiers should normally remain Latin:

```text
GammaDesk 5.0
ITIL 4
API v2
2026
```

Persian prose may use the site's preferred Persian number style where appropriate.

The choice should be consistent across the website.

---

## Dates

Dates displayed to Persian users should follow the site's chosen Persian date convention.

If date localization is implemented, it should be centralized rather than implemented independently in components.

For example:

```text
Published: ۱۲ شهریور ۱۴۰۵
```

The underlying content data should remain structured and machine-readable.

Do not store formatted dates as the primary data representation.

---

## Currency and Regional Formatting

If the website displays prices or other localized values, formatting must be separated from the underlying data.

Do not hardcode formatted values inside reusable components.

Example:

```text
Raw value → localized presentation
```

The formatting strategy should be centralized so that future locales can use different conventions.

---

## Content Structure

Content should not contain unnecessary language-specific markup.

Prefer structured content such as:

```yaml
title:
description:
content:
```

rather than embedding language-specific UI directly into components.

When multilingual content is introduced, content should be capable of representing translations cleanly.

---

## Content Localization Strategy

The preferred future model is to associate localized content with the same logical entity.

Conceptually:

```text
Product
├── fa
│   ├── title
│   ├── description
│   └── content
│
└── en
    ├── title
    ├── description
    └── content
```

The exact Astro implementation may evolve.

Do not duplicate an entire application for each language.

---

## Translation Relationships

Translations should preserve the relationship between equivalent pages.

For example:

```text
/fa/products/gammadesk
/en/products/gammadesk
```

and:

```text
/fa/products/gammadesk/features/incident-management
/en/products/gammadesk/features/incident-management
```

A translated page should refer to the same logical product or feature.

---

## URLs

Use stable, language-neutral slugs where practical.

Preferred:

```text
/products/gammadesk
/products/gammadesk/features/incident-management
```

Avoid Persian URLs unless there is a specific SEO or business reason to use them.

If localized URL prefixes are introduced later, use a predictable structure such as:

```text
/fa/products/gammadesk
/en/products/gammadesk
```

The URL strategy must be decided consistently before multilingual pages are published.

Do not mix multiple URL localization strategies.

---

## SEO and Localization

Each language must have independent SEO metadata.

Localized pages should support:

* `lang`
* Canonical URL
* Localized title
* Localized meta description
* Open Graph metadata
* Appropriate structured data
* Language-specific content
* `hreflang` relationships when multiple languages are published

Do not copy Persian SEO metadata into English pages.

---

## Language Switcher

A language switcher should only be displayed when another language is actually available.

Do not display:

```text
فارسی | English
```

if the English version does not exist.

When English becomes available, the language switcher should:

1. Clearly identify the current language.
2. Link to the equivalent page in the other language when available.
3. Preserve the user's current content context where possible.
4. Work correctly with RTL/LTR layouts.
5. Be accessible by keyboard and screen readers.

---

## Navigation Localization

Navigation labels are content and must be localizable.

For example:

```text
Persian:
محصولات
منابع
شرکت
تماس با ما

English:
Products
Resources
Company
Contact Us
```

Do not hardcode translated navigation labels inside multiple components.

Navigation data should be centralized.

---

## Component Requirements

Reusable components must not assume Persian text length.

Components should handle:

* Longer translated headings
* Different button widths
* Different navigation label lengths
* Different paragraph lengths
* RTL/LTR layout
* Mixed-direction content

Do not solve translation-specific layout problems by hardcoding widths.

Avoid:

```css
width: 120px;
```

when the width exists only to fit a particular Persian label.

Prefer content-driven sizing.

---

## Icons and Direction

Directional icons must respect document direction.

Examples:

* Arrow left
* Arrow right
* Back
* Forward
* Previous
* Next
* Breadcrumb separators

An icon that communicates direction should either:

* Automatically adapt to RTL/LTR, or
* Explicitly indicate its intended direction.

Do not assume that `left` always means "back".

---

## Images

Images containing text should be treated carefully.

Avoid embedding important marketing or informational text directly into images.

If an image contains language-specific text, provide localized versions when necessary.

Prefer HTML text over text baked into images.

---

## Accessibility

Localization must preserve accessibility.

Requirements include:

* Correct `lang` attribute
* Correct `dir` attribute
* Semantic HTML
* Accessible navigation
* Accessible language switcher
* Correct heading hierarchy
* Keyboard navigation
* Visible focus states
* Meaningful alternative text
* Proper handling of mixed-direction content

Screen readers must receive the correct language information.

---

## Content Fallback

When multiple languages exist, a missing translation must not silently produce an unrelated-language page.

Preferred behavior:

1. Show the requested language when available.
2. If a translation is intentionally unavailable, handle the page according to the site's defined fallback policy.
3. Do not pretend that untranslated content is translated.
4. Avoid duplicate indexable pages containing identical content under different language URLs.

The fallback policy must be explicit before multilingual publishing.

---

## Current Implementation

For the initial version:

* Persian is the only public language.
* All pages use `fa-IR`.
* All pages use RTL.
* Content is written in Persian.
* Components must be RTL-safe.
* CSS should use logical properties where practical.
* No complex translation framework is required.
* No language switcher is required.
* No English content needs to be created yet.

The codebase should nevertheless avoid architectural decisions that make adding English difficult later.

---

## Recommended Astro Structure

The exact implementation may evolve, but the project should be able to support a structure similar to:

```text
src/
├── components/
├── layouts/
├── pages/
├── content/
├── data/
├── styles/
└── utils/
```

If localized routes are introduced later, Astro may use a structure such as:

```text
src/pages/
├── fa/
│   ├── index.astro
│   ├── products/
│   ├── resources/
│   └── company/
│
└── en/
    ├── index.astro
    ├── products/
    ├── resources/
    └── company/
```

However, do not introduce this structure prematurely if the current single-language implementation does not require it.

---

## Translation Keys

Do not introduce large numbers of abstract translation keys for marketing content prematurely.

Avoid turning normal content into structures such as:

```text
home.hero.title
home.hero.description
home.hero.button.primary
```

unless there is a real multilingual requirement.

For the current Persian-only site, content should remain readable and maintainable.

Translation keys are more appropriate for:

* UI labels
* System messages
* Reusable interface terminology
* Navigation
* Accessibility labels
* Form messages

Long-form marketing content should remain content, not become a giant translation-key dictionary.

---

## Language-Agnostic Components

Components should receive content as data rather than embedding Persian text internally.

Prefer:

```astro
<Button>{label}</Button>
```

over:

```astro
<Button>درخواست دمو</Button>
```

This is especially important for:

* Buttons
* Navigation
* Breadcrumbs
* Forms
* Cards
* Pagination
* Search
* FAQ
* Footer
* Accessibility labels

---

## Localization and Design System

Localization requirements are part of the design system.

The design system must support:

* RTL
* LTR
* Persian typography
* Latin typography
* Mixed-direction content
* Variable text lengths
* Logical spacing
* Direction-aware icons
* Responsive navigation

Visual decisions must not make Persian support an afterthought.

---

## Localization and Performance

Localization must not unnecessarily increase JavaScript or client-side processing.

Because the website is primarily static:

* Prefer build-time localization.
* Generate localized pages statically.
* Avoid client-side translation.
* Avoid runtime translation libraries unless required.
* Do not load unused language resources on every page.

Localization should preserve the performance characteristics of the static Astro architecture.

---

## Localization and Maintainability

The localization architecture should make it easy to:

* Add English later
* Update Persian content
* Keep translations synchronized
* Identify missing translations
* Maintain SEO metadata
* Maintain navigation
* Maintain content relationships

Do not optimize for a large number of languages that the business does not currently need.

---

## Rules for the Agent

When implementing the website:

1. Treat Persian as the current primary language.
2. Use `fa-IR` for the current site.
3. Use RTL for Persian pages.
4. Keep components language-agnostic where practical.
5. Prefer CSS logical properties.
6. Do not hardcode direction assumptions into reusable components.
7. Do not create English content unless requested.
8. Do not add a language switcher until another language exists.
9. Do not introduce a complex i18n framework without a real requirement.
10. Keep content separate from presentation.
11. Keep navigation labels centralized.
12. Keep localization-aware formatting centralized.
13. Make directional icons RTL/LTR aware.
14. Ensure SEO metadata can be localized later.
15. Avoid Persian text baked into images whenever possible.
16. Ensure accessibility works correctly in RTL.
17. Do not create duplicate application structures merely to support future languages.
18. Prefer static/build-time localization over client-side translation.
19. Do not compromise the current Persian UX merely to support hypothetical future languages.
20. When English is introduced, preserve the same information architecture and content relationships.

---

## Core Principle

Persian is the product language today.

Internationalization is an architectural capability for tomorrow, not a feature that should complicate today's implementation.

Build the website so that English can be added cleanly later, while keeping the current Persian experience simple, fast, natural, and fully native.
